After the merge, the iPad holds the whole team's catalog. In this final
tutorial, Heru checks every tissue into one freezer box and exports one Darwin
Core Archive.

## 1. Check all tissues into one box

Both catalogers' tubes go into the same freezer box. On the iPad, follow
[Basic tutorial 8](/en/tutorials/basic/tissue-curation/):

{% steps %}

1. Open `Curation` from the dashboard tools.
2. In `Setup`, set `Tissue type` to `Liver`, `Preservation` to `ETOH`, and
   `Museum permanent` to `EXM`.
3. Under `Scan`, enter each tissue ID and press Enter: `Z26239`, `Z26240`,
   `Z26241`, `Z26242`, `Z26243`, `Z26245`, `Z26246`, `Z26247`, `Z26248`, and
   `Z26250`.

{% /steps %}

Scan the tubes in the order they sit on the bench—here, by tissue number. On
paper, `HH446` has `Z26241` and `HH447` has `Z26240`, so their cells follow the
tubes, not the field numbers. `Z26244` and `Z26249` belong to specimens this
series does not use.

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-curation-box.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Specimen curation on an iPad with Box 1 row A filled by tubes 6239 to 6243 and 6245 to 6248, tube 6250 in B1, and Z26250 placed in Box 1: B1"
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad" /%}

Heru's tubes fill `A1` to `A5`, Bima's Line 1 tubes `A6` to `A9`, and the tube
from his own Line 2 goes to `B1`. Curation does not care which device or
workflow produced a specimen; after the merge, every tube is in one project.

## 2. Export one Darwin Core Archive

{% steps %}

1. From the `Dashboard`, open the menu and select `Bundle records`.
2. Choose `Darwin Core Archive` as `Bundle format` and name the file
   `tujuh-teamwork-2014`.
3. Check `Package contents`, then select `Create bundle`.

{% /steps %}

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-bundle-dwca.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Bundle records on an iPad set to Darwin Core Archive, with package contents meta.xml, eml.xml, occurrence.csv with 10 records, material.csv with 20 records, and measurement_or_fact.csv with 50 records"
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad" /%}

The archive has 10 occurrences, 20 material rows (a whole body and a liver per
specimen), and 50 measurements (five per specimen). In `occurrence.csv`, records from
the shared line point to the same events, Bima's Line 2 record points to his own
event, and `recordedBy` lists each specimen's own
collector first, followed by the rest of the event team (separated by `|`):

| `catalogNumber` | `scientificName` | `eventID` ends with | `recordedBy` starts with |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `HH445` | *Mus crociduroides* | `TJHH-L1-2014-09-12` | `Heru Handika` |
| `BS1` | *Crocidura neglecta* | `TJHH-P1-2014-09-12` | `Bima Sampel` |
| `BS5` | *Maxomys hylomyoides* | `TJBS-L2-2014-09-12` | `Bima Sampel` |

For a full walk-through of the archive, see
[Basic tutorial 9](/en/tutorials/basic/export-darwin-core/).

## What you gained over separate paper catalogs

- One person entered the project, people, sites, and events once; the other
  device copied them in minutes instead of retyping them.
- In Workflow A, both catalogs link to the same site and event records, so the
  merge added specimens without duplicating a single site, event, or person.
- In Workflow B, a cataloger's own line traveled with their catalog and arrived
  in the merge as new records, with no extra steps.
- Separate field-ID series kept each cataloger's numbers on their own labels,
  with no clashes.
- After the merge, tissue curation and export treat the team's work as one
  catalog.

